## 11. Cycle-6 decision — can §4.3 supply the missing margin? **No.**

The cycle-5 verdict (item 3) requires that §4.3 be promoted from optional contingency to a required
test-only follow-up **only if** it can be supported with a literal allowlist, an invariant map, and a
*defensible expected ≤480s focused result*. It cannot supply the third. This section shows the
arithmetic that rules it out, so the decision is checkable rather than asserted.

### 11.1 What §4.3 would remove

`atomicity/fixture_test.go:83` writes `references/info.md` (content `"ref"`, 3 bytes) into every
fixture skill. Grep over the whole `atomicity` package finds **exactly one occurrence** — the write
itself. Nothing asserts on it, so removing it is assertion-neutral. That part of §4.3 stands.

It removes **one file from each installed skill context tree**. Each fixture skill
(`fixture_test.go:75-100`) contributes four files: `SKILL.md`, `references/info.md`,
`scripts/<name>-tool`, `csk-skill.json`. The project scenario installs three skill contexts
(`skill-a` and `skill-b` into the project store, the active hybrid skill into the hybrid store), so
**three files disappear**. `runtime_roots` is `["scripts"]`, so the runtime tree never carried
`references/info.md` and gains nothing.

### 11.2 Why the saving is bounded at ~12.5%, and the required saving is 14.4–18.8%

The dominant cost is `(number of saveJournal calls) × O((7N)²)`, where `N` is the **target** count.
`references/info.md` is a *file inside* a context target, not a target of its own — so **`N` is
unchanged at 19–20** and the quadratic factor does not move at all. Only the save count shrinks.

Using the §2.2 save model (`3` saves per staging entry at `staging.go:26,33,56`, plus `2` per 32 KiB
chunk at `:141,161`; every fixture file is far under 32 KiB, so one chunk each ⇒ 5 saves per file,
3 per target):

| Quantity | Value | Note |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| targets `N` | 20 | read off the alarm frame `0x14` (§1.3) |
| saves from targets | `3 × 20 = 60` | |
| staged files `F` | 12 (floor: context files only) – 30 (ceiling: + runtime, shims, env, ledgers) | statically bounded, not measured |
| saves from files | `5F` = 60 – 150 | |
| total saves | **120 – 210** | |
| saves removed by §4.3 | `3 files × 5 = 15` | |
| **reduction** | **15/120 = 12.5%** (best case) → **15/210 = 7.1%** (worst case) | |

Applying the **best case, 12.5%**, to the measured baseline — and treating the wall as proportional
to total CPU work, which §10.4 establishes is the right model for this contention-bound package:

| Measured run | ×0.875 (best case) | ×0.93 (worst case) | ≤480s? |
| --- | ---: | ---: | :---: |
| 560.828s (best) | **490.7s** | 521.6s | **no** |
| 564.022s | **493.5s** | 524.5s | **no** |
| 591.280s (worst) | **517.4s** | 549.9s | **no** |

**Even the most favourable assumption on every free parameter leaves the best run at ~491s and the
worst at ~517s — above the 480s bar in all six cells.** And the predicted 7.1–12.5% gain is only
1.3–2.3× the measurement's own 5.4% run-to-run spread, so a single post-patch run landing under 480s
would not be distinguishable from noise.

### 11.3 The structural reason no test-only lever remains

§10.4's verbose evidence settles this. In the patched tree the twelve per-class outer subtests span
**196.25s–247.78s** while a chain's *isolated* cost is **78.02s** — roughly **×3 self-contention
inflation**. Effective concurrency is **×9.6** on an **Apple M3 Max with 12 performance cores**
(`sysctl hw.perflevel0.physicalcpu` = 12, `hw.perflevel1.physicalcpu` = 4). The package is
**contention-bound, not chain-bound**: every chain finishes within 51s of every other.

That closes the remaining test-only options:

- **More partitioning is impossible.** Each chain is already `baseline + 1 injection + final` — the
  minimum that preserves the §5 assertion set. There is nothing left to split.
- **More parallelism cannot help.** The 12 performance cores are already oversubscribed 19 ways.
  Adding concurrency lengthens each chain by exactly what it saves.
- **Less parallelism cannot help either.** Fewer concurrent chains means a longer serial tail.
- Patch B already bought its ×9.6 concurrency by spending **+125% more total CPU** (twelve baseline
  rebuilds instead of two). That trade is spent; it does not repeat.

The only lever that still moves the number is **reducing total CPU work**, and the sole test-only
instance of that is §4.3 at ≤12.5% — §11.2. Everything else that would reduce work (fewer skills,
fewer agents, fewer classes, a shorter sweep) is a coverage cut that §5 and the §4.0 allowlist exist
to forbid.

### 11.4 Disposition

**§4.3 is NOT promoted to a required patch target.** It stays exactly where §4.3 already puts it:
assertion-neutral, genuinely a saving, not counted in any margin, and not worth widening the 13-file
allowlist to a 14th file for a gain that cannot reach the pass condition. A producer may still adopt
it opportunistically *after* the §6 fix lands, with a `StagingEntries` before/after measurement
attached, but it must not be presented as the thing that closes this gate.

