## 12. Cycle-6 conclusion — the test-only boundary is exhausted

This is the cycle-5 verdict's required item 4, taken deliberately.

**Patch A is done.** `internal/install` under `-race` is **226.191–235.124s** across three runs, all
exit 0, against a 600s alarm. §4.1's 88/19 split is confirmed correct and should be kept as the
recommended patch for that package. Nothing further is needed there.

**Patch B is a real improvement that does not reach the bar.** It takes
`internal/install/atomicity` from a hard timeout (`FAIL 603.701s`) to three green runs at
**560.828–591.280s**. But every run misses §7's 480s pass condition, the worst-case margin against
the 600s alarm (**8.72s**) is inside the measurement's own 5.4% spread, and §7's bar exists precisely
because focused single-package numbers read optimistically against
`go test -count=1 -race ./...`, where `cmd/curator` (**557.779s** under race) overlaps for nearly the
whole run. **On this evidence the patched atomicity package should still be expected to time out
under the real gate.**

**No remaining test-only lever closes the gap.** §11.2 bounds the only candidate at ≤12.5% against a
required 14.4–18.8%; §11.3 shows why the package's contention-bound shape leaves nothing else.

**Therefore: the required next work is the production-side fix in §6**, opened as its own story —
hoist the target-namespace independence validation out of `Engine.saveJournal`, or memoise it per
journal. §6 already states the design, the two options, and why it needs its own review and
conformance pass. Expected effect is an order of magnitude on the same hot path, which also protects
`cmd/curator` — currently at **557.779s** of a 600s alarm and the next package in line to fail.

**Explicitly not sanctioned, at any point on this ladder:** a `-timeout` override, a build tag that
excludes the sweep, skipping cases, weakening assertions, or reporting an 8.72s focused margin as a
solved race gate.

